Consider this code:

```csharp
struct Position
{
    public int X;
}

static void Move(Position first, ref Position second)
{
    first.X += 5;
    second.X += 5;
}

var a = new Position { X = 2 };
var b = a;
Move(a, ref b);
```

Explain the final values of `a.X` and `b.X`. Why does changing a field inside `Move` affect only one caller variable?

A developer expects this to change `score` from `20` to `21`:

```csharp
static void Increase(out int value)
{
    value = value + 1;
}

int score = 20;
Increase(out score);
```

Explain why the method fails to compile even though `score` is initialized. Why would changing both occurrences of `out` to `ref` make this operation valid?

Consider two parameters referring to the same caller variable:

```csharp
static void Observe(in int view, ref int editor)
{
    editor = 9;
    Console.WriteLine(view);
}

int number = 4;
Observe(in number, ref number);
```

Explain what is printed and why. Would adding `view = 12;` inside the method compile? Explain how these results fit together.
